  "worked_solution": "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7-2{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+10)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6{t}^{2},\\,6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-6{t}^{2})^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =6t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}6t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+1$, $\\text{d}u=2t\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2t\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2(8-1) \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$",
